      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-50667",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following argument regarding the divergence of the series $\u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)\\frac{1}{n + 5}$.\n\n1. Let $a_{n} = \\frac{1}{n + 5}$. Note that $a_{n} > 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$, so we can use the comparison test.\n2. The series $\u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)b_{n}$ is divergent, and $b_{n} > 0$ for all $n \\ge 1$.\n3. $a_{n} \\ge b_{n}$, for all $n \\ge 1$.\n4. Therefore, by the comparison test, $\u2211_(n = 1)^(\u221e)\\frac{1}{n + 5}$ is divergent.\n\nWhich of the following sequences could be used for $b_{n}$ in steps 2 and 3 to make the argument valid?\n\n1. $b_{n} = \\frac{1}{6n}$\n2. $b_{n} = \\frac{1}{21n}$\n3. $b_{n} = \\frac{1}{n}$"
    }
